Subject: [PATCH] pgtk: fix popup windows getting blanked ~1s after display on HiDPI

On Wayland with a GDK monitor scale factor != 1 (HiDPI, fractional
scaling), newly displayed popup/child frames briefly render correctly
and then get replaced by background color until the user triggers a
redraw.  Thereafter only cursor-adjacent regions repaint.

Root cause
----------
Each pgtk frame starts a 1-second ATIMER_CONTINUOUS in
`update_watched_scale_factor' (src/pgtkfns.c).  The watcher compares
FRAME_SCALE_FACTOR (f) against `watched_scale_factor', which is
hard-initialised to 1.0 at frame creation.  On any monitor with scale
!= 1, the first tick of the atimer sees a "change" and calls

    pgtk_cr_update_surface_desired_size (f, w, h, /*force=*/true);

With force=true, that routine unconditionally destroys the Cairo
drawing context and sets SET_FRAME_GARBAGED, even though the surface
dimensions did not change.

Before the next full redisplay commits a repaint, any partial update
path (cursor blink, mouse-face, fringe update) goes through
`pgtk_begin_cr_clip', which allocates a *new empty* Cairo surface,
draws just the small updated region, and then `flip_cr_context' in
`pgtk_frame_up_to_date' promotes that mostly-empty surface to be the
one `pgtk_handle_draw' blits to screen.  Result: the window looks
blank except for whatever Emacs happened to redraw last.

This matches the reported symptoms exactly (appears fine, blanks
within ~1s, later only the cursor area repaints).  It is
Wayland-and-HiDPI-specific because xterm.c has no equivalent watcher
and a scale=1 display never triggers the mismatch.

Fix
---
1. Root cause: in `update_watched_scale_factor', track whether we have
   ever observed the real scale factor.  The hard-coded 1.0 at init is
   a placeholder, not a prior value, so the first-time transition from
   1.0 to the actual scale should just be recorded, not treated as a
   change that requires destroying the drawing surface.  Subsequent
   genuine scale changes (e.g. dragging the frame between monitors)
   still force recreation as before.

2. Defense in depth: in `pgtk_begin_cr_clip', seed a freshly-allocated
   Cairo surface with the contents of the current `cr_active' surface
   using CAIRO_OPERATOR_SOURCE.  This guarantees that if a partial
   update beats the full garbage redisplay to the flip point, the
   "new" surface already carries the on-screen pixels, so users see
   old content instead of blank until the real repaint arrives.

Files
-----
  src/pgtkterm.h : add scale_factor_seen bit-field to pgtk_output.
  src/pgtkfns.c  : initialise the flag; gate the force on it.
  src/pgtkterm.c : seed new Cairo surface from current cr_active.

Apply with: patch -p1 < pgtk-wayland-popup-blank-fix.patch
---
 src/pgtkfns.c  | 17 ++++++++++++++---
 src/pgtkterm.c | 14 ++++++++++++++
 src/pgtkterm.h |  1 +
 3 files changed, 29 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff -u a/src/pgtkfns.c b/src/pgtkfns.c
--- a/src/pgtkfns.c
+++ b/src/pgtkfns.c
@@ -1121,11 +1121,21 @@

   if (scale_factor != FRAME_X_OUTPUT (f)->watched_scale_factor)
     {
+      bool first = !FRAME_X_OUTPUT (f)->scale_factor_seen;
       FRAME_X_OUTPUT (f)->watched_scale_factor = scale_factor;
-      pgtk_cr_update_surface_desired_size (f,
-					   FRAME_CR_SURFACE_DESIRED_WIDTH (f),
-					   FRAME_CR_SURFACE_DESIRED_HEIGHT (f),
-					   true);
+      FRAME_X_OUTPUT (f)->scale_factor_seen = true;
+
+      /* Skip forcing surface recreation on the first observation:
+	 the initial 1.0 stored at frame creation is a placeholder,
+	 not a prior scale factor that actually changed.  Destroying
+	 the Cairo context here would blank the frame until the next
+	 full redisplay, letting an intervening partial update (cursor
+	 blink, mouse-face) flip an empty surface to screen.  */
+      if (!first)
+	pgtk_cr_update_surface_desired_size (f,
+					     FRAME_CR_SURFACE_DESIRED_WIDTH (f),
+					     FRAME_CR_SURFACE_DESIRED_HEIGHT (f),
+					     true);
     }
 }

@@ -1723,6 +1733,7 @@
   FRAME_X_OUTPUT (f)->cr_surface_visible_bell = NULL;
   FRAME_X_OUTPUT (f)->atimer_visible_bell = NULL;
   FRAME_X_OUTPUT (f)->watched_scale_factor = 1.0;
+  FRAME_X_OUTPUT (f)->scale_factor_seen = false;
   struct timespec ts = make_timespec (1, 0);
   FRAME_X_OUTPUT (f)->scale_factor_atimer = start_atimer(ATIMER_CONTINUOUS,
 							 ts,
diff -u a/src/pgtkterm.c b/src/pgtkterm.c
--- a/src/pgtkterm.c
+++ b/src/pgtkterm.c
@@ -7547,6 +7547,20 @@

       cr = FRAME_CR_CONTEXT (f) = cairo_create (surface);
       cairo_surface_destroy (surface);
+
+      /* Seed the new surface with the currently-presented one so that
+	 a partial update cannot flip a mostly-empty surface to screen
+	 before a full redisplay of a GARBAGED frame completes.  */
+      if (FRAME_CR_ACTIVE_CONTEXT (f))
+	{
+	  cairo_surface_t *old
+	    = cairo_get_target (FRAME_CR_ACTIVE_CONTEXT (f));
+	  cairo_save (cr);
+	  cairo_set_operator (cr, CAIRO_OPERATOR_SOURCE);
+	  cairo_set_source_surface (cr, old, 0, 0);
+	  cairo_paint (cr);
+	  cairo_restore (cr);
+	}
     }

   cairo_save (cr);
diff -u a/src/pgtkterm.h b/src/pgtkterm.h
--- a/src/pgtkterm.h
+++ b/src/pgtkterm.h
@@ -449,6 +449,7 @@
      and cairo_surface_t.  I need GTK's such signal, but there isn't, so
      I watch it periodically with atimer. */
   double watched_scale_factor;
+  bool_bf scale_factor_seen : 1;
   struct atimer *scale_factor_atimer;
 };

